 - 0.275.3 (in place, 2026-09-04): **a hold the loop resolves by itself is a wait, not an inbox item.** 0.275.0 moved the heavy-lane run from a permission question to the exhaustion drain, but only relabelled the timeline; `isNeedsInputOrBlockedStatus` still listed `pending-heavy-testing`, so every held REQ sat under Needs input · Blocked and read as a validation waiting on the operator. When a status changes owner, walk every classifier that names it: the column bucketer, the timeline exclusion switch (a status that is neither pending nor excluded vanishes from the forecast without a word), the calendar group label, and the docs that call the column an inbox. The hold now waits in Pending → Waiting by status alone.
 - [family: paired-predicate-drift] 0.294.2 (in place, 2026-09-05): **two readers of one contract drift silently, because each one passes its own tests.** The board annotated a dependency as unmet unless it reached terminal success, while the core selector had widened to source-ready (terminal success, or `claimed` carrying a `commit:`). Both were internally consistent, so nothing failed — the board simply reported zero ready and one waiting for a card the work loop was about to select. The board already parsed the field the rule needs (`CommitHash`), so the fix was a predicate, not a parser: `isDependencySourceReadyStatus` beside `isCompletedStatus`, named after the contract section it implements. When a shared vocabulary section in `../../../do-work/actions/work-reference.md` gains an arm, grep for every predicate that spells out the old arm list; a reader that names one arm of a two-arm set is drift even when it compiles and passes.
 - [family: subject-not-restated-in-detail] REQ-588 (2026-09-05): **when a payload gains a structured field, the prose that carried the same fact must stop carrying it, and every test that read the fact out of the prose must move to the field.** REQ-579 added `Subject` to `VerifyFinding` and the board grouped on it, but every `Detail` still opened with the subject, so the board printed each name twice and the old test idiom `strings.Contains(finding.Detail, requestId)` kept working by accident. The fix rewrote sixteen details, moved twelve assertions to `finding.Subject == requestId`, and pinned the rule with a producer test that asserts category coverage before the rule so an empty fixture cannot pass. The terminal report, which has no heading, prints the subject itself. Locate a finding by its subject field; never by a substring of its prose. See the archived record: [REQ-588](../../../../do-work/archive/UR-124/REQ-588-make-each-verify-finding-row-read-as-one-warning-line.md#lessons-learned).
+- [family: live-queue-fixture-drift] REQ-174 (restored by REQ-185, 2026-09-05): **a browser probe that asserts on the window the chart is drawn at must pin `timeline.now`, the range, and its rows in its load handler.** Built from the live queue, the range reaches now only while a row is open, so a drained checkout fails the probe's own guards days after its last completion — and three consecutive `capture-request` runs were enough to flip the step-forward arrow's regime under it. `timelineFixedFixturePayloadScript` is the fixture, and three of its numbers are load-bearing: `rangeStart` more than thirty days before now, the 2% bound padding narrower than a seven-day screenful, and an empty `projection.queueEnd`. Same failure family as REQ-171's prose-window fixture. See the archived record: [REQ-174](../../../../do-work/archive/UR-037/REQ-174-fix-timeline-browser-tests-to-fixed-fixtures.md#lessons-learned).
